ECommerce-Microservices/
│
├── ECommerce.sln          → Solution containing all projects
│
├── src                    → All microservices and business logic
│
├── building-blocks        → Shared libraries used by multiple services
│
├── infrastructure         → Platform configuration (DB, Kafka, Redis, monitoring)
│
├── docker                 → Dockerfiles and Docker Compose for local development
│
├── k8s                    → Kubernetes deployment manifests
│
├── docs                   → Project documentation and architecture
│
├── scripts                → Automation scripts for build, migrations, setup
│
└── tests                  → Unit, integration, and end-to-end test projects


---------------------------------------------

.editorconfig

Keeps coding style consistent across all services.

Examples:

Indentation
Naming conventions
Formatting rules





Directory.Build.props

This is one of the most useful files in enterprise .NET projects.

Instead of writing the same package versions in every project:

Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore 9.0.0
Serilog 4.x
FluentValidation

You define common settings once.

Every project automatically inherits them.





global.json

Locks the .NET SDK version.

For example:

{
  "sdk": {
    "version": "9.0.100"
  }
}

Now every developer builds with the same SDK version.

------------------------------------------------

building-blocks/
│
├── SharedKernel/
├── Common/
├── Contracts/
└── EventBus/


SharedKernel → Domain-level shared code (entities, value objects, domain exceptions).
Contracts → Events, integration contracts, shared DTOs for inter-service communication.
EventBus → Kafka abstractions and messaging infrastructure.
Common → Truly generic utilities and extensions. Avoid putting domain logic or business rules here.




